[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Watching a video won\'t make you a hardware hacker. Fixing this robot will. This is Project Reboot. This is how you actually become a hardware hacker. Not by watching, by doing. Project Reboot is a hardware challenge tool in the form of a broken'

[00:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'robot that has intentional vulnerabilities for you to find and exploit. It\'s designed to be progressive and fun, letting you develop knowledge and acquire real-world skills without frustration. Ever wanted to get into hardware hacking,'

[00:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'but the 2nd you saw a soldering iron, you quietly closed the tab? Or maybe you already know your UART from your I2C, but you\'ve been rewatching the same 3 tutorials for a year,'

[00:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'stuck and hopeless? Perhaps you teach this stuff, and every training board you find is either a toy or a nightmare. Well, say hello to Project Reboot. Let\'s open this up. This is the complete pack, the robot and every tool you need to bring it back online. The'

[00:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'head ships separately from the body. That\'s on purpose, so nothing is damaged in transit. So your 1st job, put the robot back together. Before we turn it on, let\'s take a look at what\'s actually in this box, because every one of these'

[01:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'tools are used on real jobs by professional hardware hackers. USB TTL adapter. This is how you talk to any device\'s hidden debug console. CH341A reader. This pulls firmware straight off a flash chip. SPI clip. Grabs onto a'

[01:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'chip without desoldering anything. Logic analyzer. Reads the raw signal of any bus. No guessing.'

[01:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Multimeter. You check voltage and continuity before you touch anything you don\'t fully understand yet. And finally, a bunch of DuPont cables, male-male, female-female, male-female. Let\'s power it on and set it up. Okay, now let\'s take a'

[01:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'closer look at the robot itself so you know exactly what you are working with. This chip in the middle is an ESP32-C3 microcontroller. That\'s the core. Everything else on the board feeds it, listens to it, or gives'

[02:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you a way in. And each way in is a challenge. To give its voice back, you use those 3 pins at the bottom. That\'s UART. To restore its senses, you read that sensor through these 2 pads right there.'

[02:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'It\'s I2C. To recover its memory, you go through that flash chip. That\'s SPI. And to reconnect it to the world, you use that antenna. That\'s Bluetooth Low Energy. Quick heads-up, in this video, there will be no spoiler. You will see the'

[02:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'challenges, not the solutions. 1st, plug in the USB-C cable to power the robot. The power switch is on the left side, so you flip it to the on position, and the screen turns on. By default, you will see your progress. Top left, how many'

[02:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'challenges you completed out of 4 hearts. You earn a heart every time you finish a challenge. Top right, your mode, N for normal, H for hard. Below that, the status of all 4 challenges, UART, I2C, SPI, and Bluetooth Low Energy. There are 2 buttons.'

[03:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The page button sits above the reset button. When you press the page button, you\'ll cycle through more screens. 1st, the eyes, which changes as you complete more challenges. Then a QR code that will direct you to the manual and walkthrough'

[03:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'link. So scan that QR code with your phone, or if you want to follow along with your PC, go to this link. The walkthrough is built to guide you. Each section has a few optional questions, and I actually recommend doing them.'

[03:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'They\'re how you check whether you really understood the theory before you put it into practice on the robot. Your progress saves automatically as you go, and there\'s a reset button on the site if you ever want a clean start. One quick note, the'

[03:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'site itself also lets you toggle between an easy, hard, or combined view, but that\'s just how you want to read it. The robot only ever calls its own mode, normal or hard. If you want everything set up before you dive in, the chapter 1.5 walks'

[04:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you through it. My recommendation, run Linux on a virtual machine. Tsurugi Linux is our favorite distro for this. It comes preloaded with all the tools you need. It\'s easier, it\'s more reliable, and honestly, it\'s the OS real pentesters'

[04:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'actually use for a reason. That said, the guide\'s got you covered either way. It shows you the right tools for your own operating system whenever a challenge needs them. So here\'s the story. This robot had a very bad day. A crash brought'

[04:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'its main systems. The core is still alive, but it won\'t just reboot. Every time you solve one of the 4 challenges underneath,'

[04:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a heart lights up on this screen, and the robot\'s eyes wake up a little more. 4 hearts, 4 challenges, UART, I2C, SPI, and Bluetooth Low Energy. Our friends at Mindstorm Security designed this.'

[04:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'They\'re a team in Italy who hack hardware for a living and train pentesters too. This robot came straight out of their own training work. It was built to push offensive security professionals. But here\'s the thing. You don\'t need to be a'

[05:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'professional to use it. It teaches the exact same skills to anyone starting from 0. 1st challenge, UART. This is the low-level console hiding under the hood. The walkthrough takes you from finding the header to reading the boot log to'

[05:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'triggering a hidden shell. Inside it, a fake file system with the 1st clue. There, you\'ll learn reading serial framing, calculating baud rate, and finding hidden debug shell. Tools, USB TTL adapter, logic analyzer, capture software, serial'

[05:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'terminal. But that\'s only the surface. 2nd challenge, I2C.[upbeat music] Clip the logic analyzer onto the bus, and the walkthrough shows you exactly how to capture it. Hidden in there is a message'

[05:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the robot\'s secretly sending to its own display, and that\'s where the flag is. In this chapter, you will learn I2C addressing, sniffing a live bus, and pulling ASCII out of raw bytes. Tools, logic analyzer and I2C decoder. Still not'

[06:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'enough. The real prize is buried one step deeper. 3rd challenge, SPI. Clip onto the flash chip, pull the firmware off with the CH341A reader. The walkthrough show the'

[06:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'exact commands. The flag is hiding inside the firmware itself. In this section, you\'ll learn dumping flash in circuit, extracting a file system, reverse engineering a full binary. Tools, SOP8 clip, CH341A reader, flash ROM, pin walk,'

[06:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and a disassembler. One challenge left, and this time, it\'s not even wired. Finally, last challenge, Bluetooth low energy. Talk to the robot the same way its'

[06:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'own remote control would. The walkthrough walks you through connecting, changing modes, and sending flags straight through its packets. In this final challenge, you\'ll learn the GATT model, reading advertising data, writing and reading'

[07:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'characteristics. Tools, a BLE Explorer app or command line BLE tool. Every one of these has an easy and a hard mode. And if you get stuck at any point, there\'s a full guide waiting for you, written by Lab401. And it\'s not just hints. It walks you'

[07:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'through the real techniques hardware hackers actually use, step by step, until you truly understand each one. And here it is. The robot\'s eyes are wide open. Every heart earned, every challenge solved. This isn\'t just a training exercise'

[07:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'either. UART consoles, I2C buses, SPI flash chips, BLE devices, these show up any time you check an IoT device, an embedded system, or anything with a'

[07:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'circuit board and a battery. Most training boards teach you one interface and call it a day. This one won\'t let you fake your way past any of the 4. That\'s the difference between a toy and something MindStorm'

[08:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'actually uses to train pen testers. Lab401 is the worldwide exclusive distributor and manufacturer of Project Reboot. It comes in 2 packs. If you already own a logic analyzer and a CH341A reader, just get the basic pack, just the robot. So'

[08:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you\'re paying for the puzzle, not the tools. And if you\'re starting from 0, get the complete pack. Everything you saw, all in one box. Either way, it pays for itself the 1st time you use that logic analyzer on something other than this'

[08:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'robot. And honestly, even if you never buy either pack, I think it\'s worth bookmarking the walkthrough on its own. It really gets through the theory behind every one of these 4 protocols, and you\'ll come away understanding way more than you'

[08:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'did going in. Link to Project Reboot and the full walkthrough are in the description below. [screen whooshing] Thank you for watching, and see you next time.'
